WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.715
This chapter covers the artificial airways, from the simplest to the definitive, and what to do when one fails.

2
00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:13.700
We start with the transtracheal oxygen catheter.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:19.511
Then the basic adjuncts, the oropharyngeal and nasopharyngeal airways.

4
00:00:19.511 --> 00:00:28.393
Then the supraglottic rescue airways: the laryngeal mask airway, the King laryngeal tube and the Combitube.

5
00:00:28.393 --> 00:00:32.710
Then tracheostomy tubes and the tracheostomy button.

6
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:41.925
We finish with emergencies: a blocked tube, an accidental extubation or decannulation, and a failed intubation.

7
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:49.457
No deck in the vault covers this, so it comes from Hess chapter twenty five.

8
00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:55.903
A transtracheal oxygen catheter is placed surgically to bypass the upper airway.

9
00:00:55.903 --> 00:01:01.624
It goes through a small hole in the front of the neck into the trachea.

10
00:01:01.624 --> 00:01:16.128
It is used to conserve oxygen: because the upper airway dead space is bypassed, the patient gets the same effect as continuous-flow oxygen by nasal cannula at a lower flow setting.

11
00:01:16.128 --> 00:01:24.186
Hess lists it with the oxygen conserving devices and classes it as a low-flow, variable FiO2 device.

12
00:01:24.186 --> 00:01:29.988
Another benefit is better adherence, because it can be hidden from view.

13
00:01:29.988 --> 00:01:44.733
One trap: Hess's emergency needle cricothyrotomy, a twelve to sixteen gauge catheter through the cricothyroid membrane, is a different device, and Hess does not call it transtracheal.

14
00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:51.608
An oropharyngeal airway keeps the tongue off the back of the pharynx.

15
00:01:51.608 --> 00:01:56.924
Use it only in an unconscious patient without an intact gag reflex.

16
00:01:56.924 --> 00:02:08.667
In a conscious or semi-conscious patient with a gag reflex, it causes gagging, vomiting and aspiration, so consider a nasopharyngeal airway instead.

17
00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:19.537
It also serves as a bite block, it may make bag-valve-mask ventilation more effective, and it does not enter the larynx or the esophagus.

18
00:02:19.537 --> 00:02:27.233
There are two types: the Berman, rigid throughout, and the Guedel, reinforced in the bite region.

19
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:37.124
RCP 203 sizes it from the corner of the mouth to the angle of the jaw, by anatomy, not by age alone.

20
00:02:37.124 --> 00:02:44.171
Hess sizes it on the cheek, with the tip no farther than the ear, but use the deck.

21
00:02:44.171 --> 00:02:54.274
Too large may press the epiglottis against the larynx or traumatize the airway, and too small may push the tongue back.

22
00:02:54.274 --> 00:03:07.944
In an adult, insert it curved upward, advance to the base of the tongue, then rotate it one hundred eighty degrees, or insert it from the side and rotate ninety.

23
00:03:07.944 --> 00:03:11.765
In infants and small children, do not rotate.

24
00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:18.727
RCP 203 says to depress the tongue with a tongue depressor and insert it directly.

25
00:03:18.727 --> 00:03:26.283
And do not tape over the center opening, because that is the path for a suction catheter.

26
00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.537
The nasopharyngeal airway is the alternative.

27
00:03:31.537 --> 00:03:45.435
It can be used in conscious or semi-conscious patients with an intact gag reflex, and it is better tolerated because it does not stimulate the back of the pharynx.

28
00:03:45.435 --> 00:03:50.465
Its tip rests behind the tongue, just above the epiglottis.

29
00:03:50.465 --> 00:04:04.107
It bypasses an upper airway obstruction, helps pass a bronchoscope, and serves as a path for nasotracheal suctioning, with less trauma from repeated suctioning.

30
00:04:04.107 --> 00:04:13.827
RCP 203 sizes it from the nares to the earlobe or tragus, choosing the largest diameter that passes without force.

31
00:04:13.827 --> 00:04:24.571
Lubricate it with water-soluble jelly and advance it along the floor of the nose, bevel toward the septum, and never force it.

32
00:04:24.571 --> 00:04:32.671
The major contraindication is a suspected basilar skull fracture or significant midface trauma.

33
00:04:32.671 --> 00:04:40.600
Complications are laryngospasm, coughing, nosebleeds, and sinus infection with prolonged use.

34
00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:43.437
Pause and decide.

35
00:04:43.437 --> 00:04:50.124
A patient does not arouse but does cough, and no intubation equipment is at hand.

36
00:04:50.124 --> 00:04:57.058
Which basic airway do you choose, and what is the one injury that would rule it out?

37
00:04:57.967 --> 00:05:03.967
[6-second practice pause]

38
00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:07.295
Choose a nasopharyngeal airway.

39
00:05:07.295 --> 00:05:17.972
The cough tells you a gag reflex is present, so an oropharyngeal airway would cause gagging, vomiting and aspiration.

40
00:05:17.972 --> 00:05:22.352
The nasal airway is tolerated with a gag reflex.

41
00:05:22.352 --> 00:05:26.549
This is the deck's own stop and think example.

42
00:05:26.549 --> 00:05:35.492
The injury that rules it out is a suspected basilar skull fracture, or significant midface trauma.

43
00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:47.281
The laryngeal mask airway is a supraglottic airway: a tube and mask with an inflatable cuff, inserted blindly into the pharynx.

44
00:05:47.281 --> 00:05:55.047
The mask seals around the laryngeal inlet above the glottis, and it does not enter the trachea.

45
00:05:55.047 --> 00:06:11.722
It is used for routine airway management during general anesthesia and as a rescue device in a difficult or emergency airway, and it ventilates better than a bag-valve-mask with less gastric insufflation.

46
00:06:11.722 --> 00:06:18.425
Its main limit is less protection from aspiration than a cuffed endotracheal tube.

47
00:06:19.833 --> 00:06:31.788
The RCP 203 deck sizes the laryngeal mask airway by weight: size four for fifty to seventy kilograms, size five for seventy to one hundred.

48
00:06:31.788 --> 00:06:36.174
Hess sizes it by build, but use the deck's weights.

49
00:06:36.174 --> 00:06:45.290
The cuff can go up to sixty centimeters of water, compared with twenty to thirty for an endotracheal tube.

50
00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:52.944
With positive pressure, squeeze the bag slowly and keep pressures below twenty to thirty.

51
00:06:52.944 --> 00:06:57.847
If it leaks, check the position; don't just add cuff air.

52
00:06:57.847 --> 00:07:04.211
Secure it with a bite block, not an oral airway, which causes malposition.

53
00:07:04.211 --> 00:07:08.167
If the patient regurgitates, do not remove it.

54
00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:17.283
Tilt the patient head-down or onto the side, disconnect the ventilation, suction, and prepare to intubate.

55
00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:22.113
The chapter fifteen deck gives a ladder.

56
00:07:22.113 --> 00:07:25.697
Oral and nasal airways are basic adjuncts.

57
00:07:25.697 --> 00:07:34.316
The King laryngeal tube, the Combitube and the laryngeal mask airway are supraglottic rescue airways.

58
00:07:34.316 --> 00:07:39.094
The endotracheal tube is the definitive tracheal airway.

59
00:07:39.094 --> 00:07:45.324
All three rescue airways are placed blindly, not through the vocal cords.

60
00:07:45.324 --> 00:07:52.235
The King LT has two cuffs, one inflation line that fills both, and one connector.

61
00:07:52.235 --> 00:07:55.819
Its tip normally ends up in the esophagus.

62
00:07:55.819 --> 00:08:08.534
The distal cuff blocks the esophagus, the proximal cuff seals the pharynx, and gas leaves through openings between the cuffs, in front of the larynx.

63
00:08:08.534 --> 00:08:16.555
The standard model dead-ends at the esophageal cuff, and the LT-S adds a gastric access lumen.

64
00:08:16.555 --> 00:08:21.675
Both pass an airway exchange catheter or a fiberoptic scope.

65
00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:29.788
The esophageal tracheal Combitube has two lumens, two cuffs and two connectors.

66
00:08:29.788 --> 00:08:35.714
The blue pharyngeal lumen has a large cuff, side holes and a dead end.

67
00:08:35.714 --> 00:08:40.032
The clear lumen has a regular cuff and an open tip.

68
00:08:40.032 --> 00:08:51.546
The Combitube usually lands in the esophagus, so ventilate through the blue lumen first, and gas exits the side holes toward the larynx.

69
00:08:51.546 --> 00:09:01.028
If the tip went into the trachea instead, switch to the clear lumen, which then works like an endotracheal tube.

70
00:09:01.028 --> 00:09:07.377
The exam anchor is blue first: esophagus, blue lumen; trachea, clear lumen.

71
00:09:07.377 --> 00:09:14.742
Then confirm with chest rise, breath sounds on both sides and end-tidal carbon dioxide.

72
00:09:14.742 --> 00:09:23.970
The King LT has no blue-first decision, because it has one connector and is designed to sit in the esophagus.

73
00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:32.775
The laryngeal mask, by contrast, sits in the pyriform sinus over the laryngeal opening, above the cords.

74
00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:35.127
Pause here.

75
00:09:35.127 --> 00:09:46.589
You place a Combitube and ventilate through the blue lumen, but there is no chest rise, no breath sounds, and no exhaled carbon dioxide.

76
00:09:46.589 --> 00:09:51.225
Where is the tip most likely to be, and what do you do?

77
00:09:52.133 --> 00:09:58.133
[6-second practice pause]

78
00:09:58.633 --> 00:10:07.779
Blue first assumes the tip went into the esophagus, where gas from the blue lumen's side holes flows toward the larynx.

79
00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:18.155
If ventilating the blue lumen gives no chest rise, no breath sounds and no carbon dioxide, the tip has most likely entered the trachea.

80
00:10:18.155 --> 00:10:30.683
Switch to the clear lumen, which then works like an endotracheal tube, and confirm again with chest rise, breath sounds on both sides and end-tidal carbon dioxide.

81
00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:39.283
A tracheostomy is the surgical introduction of a tube into the trachea through the neck.

82
00:10:39.283 --> 00:10:44.017
Tracheotomy is the procedure; tracheostomy is the opening.

83
00:10:44.017 --> 00:10:57.322
There are three main reasons: to bypass an upper airway obstruction, to help remove secretions, and for long-term mechanical ventilation, probably the most common.

84
00:10:57.322 --> 00:11:04.342
Because it bypasses the upper airway, warming, humidification and filtration are lost.

85
00:11:04.342 --> 00:11:18.218
Tubes are sized by internal diameter, but fit depends on the outer diameter, which should be no more than two thirds to three quarters of the trachea's internal diameter.

86
00:11:18.218 --> 00:11:26.218
Remove the obturator right after insertion and keep it at the bedside, with a spare inner cannula.

87
00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:32.421
A speaking valve or a capped fenestrated tube needs the cuff fully deflated.

88
00:11:32.421 --> 00:11:34.625
Never cap with the cuff up.

89
00:11:36.033 --> 00:11:40.903
A tracheostomy button keeps the stoma open without a full tube.

90
00:11:40.903 --> 00:11:48.401
It is a short cannula with a flared outer end, a flanged inner end, and spacers to set the depth.

91
00:11:48.401 --> 00:11:59.146
When it is plugged, the patient breathes and coughs through the upper airway, and with a one-way valve the patient can talk, eat and cough.

92
00:11:59.146 --> 00:12:02.625
But positive pressure through a button leaks.

93
00:12:02.625 --> 00:12:07.958
It is not a secure cuffed airway, and Hess notes that it has no cuff.

94
00:12:09.367 --> 00:12:26.099
An endotracheal or tracheostomy tube can be blocked by kinking, by the patient biting the tube, by the tip resting against the tracheal wall, mainly with trach tubes, or by thick secretions, mucus or blood clots in the lumen.

95
00:12:26.099 --> 00:12:32.420
Rarely, the cuff herniates over the tip, or an overinflated cuff compresses the tube.

96
00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:44.616
The first step, if the patient is on the ventilator, is to remove the patient from the ventilator, ventilate manually with one hundred percent oxygen, and reassess.

97
00:12:44.616 --> 00:12:53.317
If one is immediately available, try passing an airway exchange catheter through the blocked tube before removing it.

98
00:12:53.317 --> 00:13:02.167
For a trach patient, you may need to cover the stoma with a petroleum gauze pad and ventilate through the upper airway.

99
00:13:03.567 --> 00:13:14.024
A partial extubation can mimic a blown cuff, so first rule out a large cuff leak by checking the cuff, the pilot tube and the valve.

100
00:13:14.024 --> 00:13:22.738
For a partial extubation, deflate the cuff, loosen the holder and try to reposition the tube into the trachea.

101
00:13:22.738 --> 00:13:32.482
Confirm by listening, by capnography or a carbon dioxide colorimeter, an esophageal detector or a scope, then a chest film.

102
00:13:32.482 --> 00:13:42.305
If that fails, remove the tube, ventilate by bag-valve-mask with oxygen, and consider a supraglottic airway or reintubation.

103
00:13:42.305 --> 00:13:54.980
If a tracheostomy tube comes out of a fresh stoma, call for help, cover the stoma with a sterile petroleum gauze pad, and bag the patient with oxygen as needed.

104
00:13:54.980 --> 00:14:06.467
With a well-established stoma in a stable patient, get a sterile tube of the same size or one size smaller and follow the trach change procedure.

105
00:14:07.867 --> 00:14:16.072
A failed intubation means the tube cannot be placed after several attempts, some sources say three.

106
00:14:16.072 --> 00:14:36.627
The signs are absent breath sounds, abdominal distention with sounds over the stomach during bagging, no exhaled carbon dioxide, breaths that are hard to deliver, a rapid fall in saturation, clinical deterioration, and stomach contents in the tube.

107
00:14:36.627 --> 00:14:42.677
Oxygen by nasal cannula during the attempts helps hold the saturation up.

108
00:14:42.677 --> 00:14:47.816
After any failed attempt, bag with one hundred percent oxygen.

109
00:14:47.816 --> 00:15:01.160
If bagging keeps the saturation above ninety percent, allow up to two more attempts by the most experienced clinician, preferably with rapid sequence intubation.

110
00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:08.371
If the saturation is below ninety or falling, you cannot intubate and cannot oxygenate.

111
00:15:08.371 --> 00:15:13.427
Insert a supraglottic airway immediately and restart bagging.

112
00:15:13.427 --> 00:15:19.892
If either pathway fails, the only alternative is an emergency surgical airway.

113
00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.352
Here is what to remember.

114
00:15:23.352 --> 00:15:42.153
An oral airway is only for a patient with no gag reflex, sized from the mouth corner to the jaw angle; a nasal airway is tolerated with a gag reflex but never with a suspected basilar skull fracture or significant midface trauma.

115
00:15:42.153 --> 00:15:50.445
The laryngeal mask seals above the glottis, is sized by weight, and protects less against aspiration.

116
00:15:50.445 --> 00:15:57.751
With a Combitube, ventilate blue first, and switch to clear if the tip is in the trachea.

117
00:15:57.751 --> 00:16:04.730
A tracheostomy must have its cuff down before it is capped, and a button has no cuff.

118
00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:12.611
For a blocked tube, take the patient off the ventilator and bag with one hundred percent oxygen.

119
00:16:12.611 --> 00:16:19.425
And when you cannot intubate and cannot oxygenate, place a supraglottic airway now.
